  "optionExplanations": {
    "A": "UTI can cause abdominal pain and fever but does not typically cause migration of pain from the umbilicus to the right lower quadrant.",
    "B": "A hernia would usually present with a groin or scrotal bulge and localized pain.",
    "C": "Mesenteric adenitis can mimic appendicitis, but the classic migratory pain pattern is more suggestive of appendicitis.",
    "D": "Correct: migratory periumbilical-to-RLQ pain with vomiting, anorexia, and fever strongly suggests acute appendicitis.",
    "E": "Gastritis usually causes epigastric pain rather than localized right lower quadrant pain."
  },
